{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_removal_confirmation_a4bd.py::test_pass_summary_reports_the_decline_count`\nfails intermittently under CI's `-n 3 --dist worksteal` invocation (it -1'd Gerrit 1715 and\n1716 in one hour, on both macOS and ubuntu shards) and passes 10/10 in isolation. The\nassertion wants the SUMMARY line `declined 1 inbound link removal`; `caplog` holds only the\nPER-ITEM line `declining the inbound removal of ...`.\n\n**Root cause (reproduced deterministically, not a hypothesis).** The two lines differ in\nLEVEL, not in code path: the per-item decline is `logger.warning` in\n`apply_inbound_records._inbound_unlink_one`, the pass summary is `logger.info` at the end of\n`apply_inbound_records._inbound_update_apply_links`. Both fire; only the INFO one can be\ndropped. Both use `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)` =\n`rebar_reconciler.apply_inbound_records`.\n\n`rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:606` calls `install_stderr_handler(\"rebar_reconciler\")`, and\n`rebar/_logging.py:88` does `logger.setLevel(level)` — pinning the shared\n**`rebar_reconciler`** root logger to WARNING, process-globally and never restored. Any test\nthat drives the reconciler entrypoint in-process (e.g.\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_main_entry.py`) leaks that level to every later\ntest in the same xdist worker. `caplog.at_level(logging.INFO)` with no `logger=` argument\nraises the **ROOT** level only, so the INFO record is discarded at the originating logger by\n`isEnabledFor` before any handler is consulted — while the WARNING record still arrives.\nThat is exactly the observed asymmetry.\n\nThe repo already documents this precise vector and already contains containment for it —\n`tests/_log_integrity.py` (`restore_level`) plus the autouse\n`_rebar_log_propagation_guard` in `tests/conftest.py` — but both are hard-coded to the single\nroot `REBAR_LOGGER_NAME = \"rebar\"`. `rebar/_logging.py`'s own docstring names **two** shared\nroots (\"the sibling `rebar_reconciler` root for the reconciler subprocess\"). The guard simply\nmisses the sibling, so reconciler-side INFO assertions are unprotected.\n\nConfirmed RED locally, first try, deterministic:\n`pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_main_entry.py tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_removal_confirmation_a4bd.py -p no:randomly`\n=> 1 failed, 15 passed, with the exact CI assertion text.\n\nThis is a TEST-INFRASTRUCTURE defect, not a production one: when the confirmation store is\n`None` the removal is not declined at all (it proceeds), so `declined == 0` and a suppressed\nsummary is correct-by-design. No production logging change is warranted.\n\n## Approach\n\nGeneralise the existing caplog coverage-integrity guard from one shared root to the set of\nshared roots, rather than papering over the symptom with a `logger=` argument on the one\nassertion. The mechanism is process-global and generic — every `rebar_reconciler.*` INFO/DEBUG\nassertion in the suite is exposed to it — so containment at the guard is the fix that closes\nthe class; a per-test tweak would leave the next such assertion to flake.\n\nIn `tests/_log_integrity.py`, replace the scalar `REBAR_LOGGER_NAME` with\n`SHARED_LOGGER_NAMES = (\"rebar\", \"rebar_reconciler\")` and make the four primitives operate\nover that tuple: `propagation_failure` reports the first non-propagating root by name,\n`restore_propagation` re-enables all of them, `current_level` returns a\n`dict[str, int]` snapshot, and `restore_level` takes that snapshot and reports whether any\nroot moved. The `tests/conftest.py` autouse fixture keeps its exact shape (snapshot at setup,\nrestore in `finally`); only the value it carries becomes a mapping. Rationale for the tuple\nover per-test opt-in: the leak is caused by legitimate code (a real entrypoint installing its\nhandler), so containment — every test starting from the same level — is what makes log\nassertions order-independent, which is the property CI needs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/_log_integrity.py` — multi-root primitives + docstring naming the reconciler root.\n- `tests/conftest.py` — the autouse guard carries the per-root level snapshot.\n- `tests/unit/test_caplog_coverage_integrity.py` — update the self-tests to the new signature\n  and add a pytester pair proving the `rebar_reconciler` root is contained (without the guard\n  a leaked reconciler level breaks a later INFO assertion; with it, green).\n\nNo `src/` change.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Failure path first (the reproduction).** Run the polluter module and the flaky module in\n  one process with `-p no:randomly`; assert it goes from 1-failed to 0-failed with the fix.\n- **Guard self-test, both directions (pytester, in\n  `tests/unit/test_caplog_coverage_integrity.py`).** New pair mirroring the existing `rebar`\n  level pair, but poisoning `logging.getLogger(\"rebar_reconciler\").setLevel(WARNING)` and then\n  asserting an INFO record from `rebar_reconciler.probe`: WITHOUT the guard conftest the later\n  test FAILS; WITH it, both pass. Worked example of the poisoned file:\n  `def test_poisoner(): logging.getLogger(\"rebar_reconciler\").setLevel(logging.WARNING)`\n  followed by `def test_info(caplog):` doing `with caplog.at_level(logging.INFO):\n  logging.getLogger(\"rebar_reconciler.probe\").info(\"hello\")` and\n  `assert any(\"hello\" in r.getMessage() for r in caplog.records)`.\n- **Edge/no-op path.** `restore_level` on an unmoved snapshot returns `False`; a snapshot\n  where only one root moved returns `True` and restores just that root.\n- **Propagation vector unchanged.** The existing propagation self-tests keep passing, and a\n  new case asserts a non-propagating `rebar_reconciler` root is also blamed by name.\n- **CI invocation.** The enclosing module plus the reconciler subtree under\n  `-n 3 --dist worksteal`, repeated, plus the full CI selection.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The order dependency is named and demonstrated: a two-module invocation\n      (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_main_entry.py` then the a4bd module,\n      `-p no:randomly`) fails BEFORE the change and passes after — both runs recorded on this\n      ticket.\n- [ ] `tests/_log_integrity.py` exposes `SHARED_LOGGER_NAMES` containing both `\"rebar\"` and\n      `\"rebar_reconciler\"`, and `tests/conftest.py`'s autouse guard restores the level of both\n      — verified by the new pytester self-test pair going red without the guard and green with\n      it (`pytest tests/unit/test_caplog_coverage_integrity.py`, all pass).\n- [ ] `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 3 --dist worksteal --timeout=300` passes\n      on the change, and the a4bd module passes across 10 consecutive\n      `-n 3 --dist worksteal` runs of `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` (run count recorded in a\n      ticket comment).\n- [ ] The `confirm_store is None` question is answered in a ticket comment with the code\n      citation: it is correct-by-design (no store => no decline => `declined == 0`), and NOT\n      the mechanism behind this flake, so no production logging change is made."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786611325143922001,"uuid":"b285a61c-55ce-4deb-a179-bc13123d450b"}